<title>CANADA: Canada appeal court overturns pasta dumping ruling.</title>
<headline>Canada appeal court overturns pasta dumping ruling.</headline>
<dateline>OTTAWA 1997-02-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Canada's Federal Court of Appeal has overturned a ruling that dumped and subsidized Italian pasta did not hurt the Canadian pasta industry, sending the case back to the Canadian International Trade Tribunal for a new hearing, a court spokesman said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;It has been referred back to the Tribunal,&quot; a court spokesman said.</p>
<p>Last May the Tribunal ruled that even though some dry pasta imported from Italy was being dumped and subsidized, this did not hurt the domestic pasta industry.</p>
<p>Following the Tribunal's ruling, Revenue Canada refunded all provisional duties it had collected on such pasta since last January.</p>
<p>The issue refers to dry Italian pasta, not stuffed or otherwise prepared and not containing eggs, in packages up to and including 2.3 kg.</p>
<p>((Reuters Ottawa Bureau, 613-235-6745))</p>
